der the stated 10% clinical-significance rule. The most informative next analysis is to test the remaining active moderate-prevalence binary screens H56 through H63, which have adequate exposed and comparator cell sizes and cover outcome_002, outcome_012, outcome_005, outcome_015, outcome_003, outcome_016, outcome_013, and outcome_011. The outcome_013 categorical screens H45 and H46 should remain active but lower priority. I defer the currently registered interaction claims H29 through H32 and H76 because their underlying main effects are largely null and interaction tests are more multiplicity-prone; they should be revived only if a main-effect candidate emerges or if main-effect screening exhausts. No further independent validation is useful now because no candidate has a threshold-magnitude estimate with a confidence interval credibly excluding clinically unimportant values, or reproducible threshold-exceeding estimates across outcomes. All conclusions are limited to observational associations in this supplied dataset and do not imply causality or generalizability."
